<h1>Queries</h1>
<p>A query asks the physics simulation a question without spawning anything: “what is along this line?”, “what sits inside this circle?”, “is anything under my feet?”. This page shows when to use each query type and every field a hit returns.</p>

<h2>What a query is</h2>
<p>Gameplay constantly needs to know about space before it acts. A turret must know whether a wall blocks its view. A hero must know whether it is standing on ground before allowing a jump. A pickup must know which loot lies within reach.</p>
<p>You could answer any of those with a trigger volume and an event, but that means extra entities and state to clean up. A <strong>query</strong> is a one-shot question answered immediately: pass positions and sizes, get back a hit (or nothing). Nothing is spawned, nothing persists, and it is safe to call every frame.</p>
<p>Queries respect the same <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-layers'}) }}">layer filters</a> as real contacts, so a ray that ignores pickups can be authored with <code>mask_bits</code> rather than extra checks in Lua.</p>

<h2>Raycast, overlap, and shape cast</h2>
<p>Three families of questions cover almost every need.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Question</th><th>Everyday uses</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Raycast</strong></td><td>What does this straight line hit first?</td><td>Line of sight, laser sights, mouse picking, sniper traces.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Overlap</strong></td><td>What is already inside this region?</td><td>Pickup radius, blast area, “is this tile occupied?”, crowd gathering.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Shape cast</strong></td><td>What would this moving shape hit on the way?</td><td>Ground check with a sphere, dash safety, projectile width sweeps.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Use a ray when you only need the first blocker. Use overlap when you need everyone inside an area. Use a cast when the moving object has real width and a thin ray would miss the wall it scrapes.</p>

<h2>2D queries</h2>
<p>The 2D module is <code>demi.physics.query2d</code>. All positions are world-space <code>x, y</code>. Raycasts take an <strong>origin plus a direction and a distance</strong>, not two end points.</p>
<pre><code>local Q2 = require("demi.physics.query2d")

-- Cast 8 units straight down from (2, 5):
local hit = Q2.raycast(2, 5, 0, -1, 8)
if hit then
    print(hit.entity_id, hit.distance)
end

local touching_ids = Q2.overlap_circle(4, 0, 1.5)          -- array of entity IDs
local shard_hits   = Q2.overlap_circle_all(4, 0, 1.5)      -- array of hit tables
local box_occupied = Q2.overlap_box(0, 0, 2, 2)            -- boolean

local touching = Q2.has_contact("player")
local points   = Q2.contacts("player")</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>raycast</code></td><td>origin <code>x, y</code>, direction <code>dir_x, dir_y</code>, <code>distance</code>, then optional <code>layer</code> and <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>One hit table, or <code>nil</code> when the ray hits nothing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_box</code></td><td>center <code>x, y</code>, size <code>width, height</code>, optional <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td><code>true</code> when anything overlaps the box, otherwise <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_circle</code></td><td>center <code>x, y</code>, <code>radius</code>, optional <code>layer</code> and <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>Array of overlapping <strong>entity IDs</strong> (empty when none).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_box_all</code></td><td>center <code>x, y</code>, size <code>width, height</code>, optional <code>layer</code> and <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>Array of hit tables. Empty when nothing is found — never <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_circle_all</code></td><td>Same optional arguments as <code>overlap_circle</code></td><td>Array of hit tables.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>contacts</code></td><td>one entity id</td><td>Array of that entity’s current contacts (with phase <code>enter/stay/exit</code>), empty when apart.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>has_contact</code></td><td>one entity id, optional filter table (<code>layer</code>, normal ranges, <code>include_triggers</code>)</td><td><code>true</code> when the entity currently touches a matching collider.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Optional <code>layer</code> narrows a query to one <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-layers'}) }}">physics layer</a>; optional <code>ignored_entity_id</code> skips one entity (the caster itself, usually).</p>

<h3>2D hit fields</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>entity_id</code></td><td>entity id</td><td>The entity whose collider was hit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>layer</code></td><td>string</td><td>Layer name of the hit collider.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>point</code></td><td><code>{ x, y }</code></td><td>World position of the contact or intersection.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td><code>{ x, y }</code></td><td>Unit vector pointing away from the surface — use it for bounce and alignment.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>distance</code></td><td>number</td><td>Distance along the ray or cast to the hit, in world units.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fraction</code></td><td>number</td><td>How far along the query the hit is, from 0 (start) to 1 (end).</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>3D hits additionally carry <code>collider_part_id</code> (stable part ID inside a compound collider, raycasts only) and <code>is_trigger</code>.</p>

<h3>Everyday 2D patterns</h3>
<p>Line of sight for a Skyforge patrol drone — does a wall sit between the drone and the intruder?</p>
<pre><code>local Q2 = require("demi.physics.query2d")

local to_player_x, to_player_y = px - drone_x, py - drone_y
local length = math.sqrt(to_player_x * to_player_x + to_player_y * to_player_y)
local dir_x, dir_y = to_player_x / length, to_player_y / length

local hit = Q2.raycast(drone_x, drone_y, dir_x, dir_y, length, nil, drone_id)
if hit and hit.entity_id ~= player_id then
    -- a wall (or something else) blocks the view
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>Normalize the direction to the player and cast that far; <code>nil</code> means the path is clear.</li>
  <li>The drone itself is skipped with <code>ignored_entity_id</code>, so it never blocks its own view.</li>
  <li>Comparing <code>hit.entity_id</code> to the player tells you whether the player was the first thing in the way.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pickup radius — grab every shard in range:</p>
<pre><code>local shards = Q2.overlap_circle_all(x, y, 1.25)
for _, h in ipairs(shards) do
    -- h.entity_id, h.point, h.distance
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>overlap_circle_all</code> returns every overlapping hit as an array of hit tables (fields in the table above).</li>
  <li>An empty array means nothing was in range; <code>ipairs</code> simply does nothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ground check before a jump:</p>
<pre><code>local ground = Q2.raycast(x, y, 0, -1, 0.2)
local can_jump = ground ~= nil</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>The ray starts at the feet, points straight down (direction <code>0, -1</code>), and reaches 0.2 units.</li>
  <li><code>can_jump</code> becomes <code>true</code> when something was hit, <code>false</code> when the result was <code>nil</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3D queries</h2>
<p>The 3D module is <code>demi.physics.query3d</code>. Rays are defined by an origin and a direction, not two points.</p>
<pre><code>local Q3 = require("demi.physics.query3d")

local hit = Q3.raycast(ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz, max_distance)
if hit then
    print(hit.entity_id, hit.distance)
end

local ids  = Q3.overlap_sphere(x, y, z, radius)          -- array of entity IDs
local hits = Q3.overlap_sphere_all(x, y, z, radius)      -- array of hit tables
local boxes = Q3.overlap_box_all(x, y, z, width, height, depth)
local cast = Q3.sphere_cast(ox, oy, oz, radius, dir_x, dir_y, dir_z, distance)</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>raycast</code></td><td>origin <code>ox, oy, oz</code>, direction <code>dx, dy, dz</code>, <code>distance</code>, optional <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>Hit table or <code>nil</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_sphere</code></td><td>center <code>x, y, z</code>, <code>radius</code>, optional <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>Array of overlapping <strong>entity IDs</strong>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_sphere_all</code></td><td>center, <code>radius</code>, optional <code>layer</code> and <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>Array of hit tables, empty when none.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>overlap_box_all</code></td><td>center <code>x, y, z</code>, size <code>width, height, depth</code>, optional <code>layer</code> and <code>ignored_entity_id</code></td><td>Array of hit tables, empty when none.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>sphere_cast</code></td><td>origin, <code>radius</code>, direction <code>dir_x, dir_y, dir_z</code>, <code>distance</code>, then optional <code>layer</code>, <code>ignored_entity_id</code>, <code>include_triggers</code></td><td>Hit table or <code>nil</code>. Sweeps the sphere along the direction.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Only <code>sphere_cast</code> accepts <code>include_triggers</code> (default <code>true</code>; pass <code>false</code> to find the first solid collider beyond triggers). The 3D raycast’s optional final argument is an <code>ignored_entity_id</code>, not a layer filter.</p>

<h3>3D hit fields</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>entity_id</code></td><td>entity id</td><td>Entity whose collider was hit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>layer</code></td><td>string</td><td>Layer name of the hit collider.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>point</code></td><td><code>{ x, y, z }</code></td><td>World position of the hit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td><code>{ x, y, z }</code></td><td>Outward surface direction at the hit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>distance</code></td><td>number</td><td>World units from the query origin to the hit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fraction</code></td><td>number</td><td>Progress along the query, 0 to 1.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Unlike 2D, <code>point</code> and <code>normal</code> are 3-component tables. A camera-driven pick in Skyforge looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>local Q3 = require("demi.physics.query3d")
local Camera3D = require("demi.camera3d")

local hit = Camera3D.screen_ray(camera_id, mx, my, view_w, view_h)
if hit then
    -- hit.entity_id, hit.point {x, y, z}, hit.distance
end</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>screen_ray</code> builds a world ray from a mouse position and casts it for you.</li>
  <li>It returns the same hit shape as <code>query3d.raycast</code>, or <code>nil</code> when the click is empty sky.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Nil versus empty array</h2>
<p>This distinction saves a class of bugs. Single-result queries and <code>nil</code> go together; list queries and empty arrays go together.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>When</th><th>How to check</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>nil</code></td><td><code>raycast</code> or <code>sphere_cast</code> found nothing</td><td><code>if hit then ... end</code> before reading <code>hit.*</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>overlap_box</code> found nothing, or <code>has_contact</code> reports no touch</td><td>Plain boolean test</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Empty array</td><td><code>overlap_*_all</code> or <code>contacts</code> found nothing</td><td><code>#list == 0</code> or simply loop with <code>ipairs</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Always guard single-hit results before reading fields: <code>hit.entity_id</code> on a <code>nil</code> hit raises an error, while iterating an empty array is harmless.</p>

<h2>Practical tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Queries are cheap enough for per-frame checks like ground tests, but do not cast hundreds of long rays every step if one area query answers the same question.</li>
  <li><code>include_triggers</code> only exists on 3D <code>sphere_cast</code> (default <code>true</code>). 2D raycasts and 3D raycasts always report whatever is first; filter by <code>hit.is_trigger</code> or <code>layer</code> when triggers should not count.</li>
  <li>Use <code>fraction</code> to lerp effects along a laser path and <code>normal</code> to orient decals and bounce particles.</li>
  <li>Layer filters decide who is visible to a query — tune them in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-layers'}) }}">Physics layers</a> instead of filtering results by hand.</li>
</ul>
<p>Queries are immediate; lasting contacts are better served by <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'physics-events'}) }}">Physics events</a>. Shapes being tested are described in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'colliders'}) }}">Colliders</a>.</p>